course: dictionary
lesson: 1483
title: Caucus, a meeting and a silly race
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A caucus is a kind of meeting. The context gives the clues: «A caucus is a meeting of members of a political party», so the word is about people who belong to a group, coming together to choose or decide something.
explain: The same word can mean different things in different places. In the USA, «the Iowa caucuses» help choose presidential candidates. In New Zealand and Australia, a party caucus is simply its members of parliament meeting together.
explain: Lewis Carroll also uses caucus as a joke in «Alice in Wonderland». Ask yourself what fits: the Caucus-race goes round in circles, starts and stops whenever it likes, and gets nowhere. The joke is that people in meetings can seem to go round in circles too.
fact: A caucus is a meeting of members of a political party or group, held to choose candidates or decide what to do.
fact: A caucus can also be a group inside an organisation who share the same interests.
fact: Caucus can be a noun (a caucus, caucuses) or a verb (to caucus, caucusing), meaning to hold a caucus meeting.
fact: In «Alice in Wonderland», the Caucus-race is a silly race round a rough circle, and at the end «Everybody has won, and all must have prizes».
fact: The Dodo in the story was a real bird from the island of Mauritius, and it died out long before Alice was written.
exercise: «The party held a caucus to choose its candidate.» What does caucus mean here? | *A meeting to choose someone | A quiet walk in the park | A kind of pigeon from an island | A race round a circle
exercise: In the Caucus-race, everybody runs round a rough circle, starting and stopping whenever they like. Why is this a joke? | Because the race is very long and nobody wins | *Because the race goes nowhere, though everyone is said to win | Because the animals are all asleep | Because the race is done in a straight line
exercise: Which group is a caucus in the second meaning, a group inside an organisation who share the same interests? | A group of school club members who all want to save the library | *A small group inside the club who all want more music lessons | A crowd of people waiting for a bus | A single pupil doing homework alone
test: «In New Zealand and Australia, a party caucus is its members of parliament meeting together.» Who meets in a party caucus? | Children from the school choir | Everybody who lives in the country | The Dodo and Alice | *Members of parliament from the same party
test: «The Dodo answers, the best way to explain it is to do it.» What does the Dodo mean? | The race is too dangerous to join | Alice should write a book about it | *You will understand the Caucus-race better by joining in | Explaining is always boring
exam: «Everybody has won, and all must have prizes.» Why is this funny in the Caucus-race? | *Because nobody really got anywhere, so the prize joke makes the race seem silly | Because the prizes are very expensive | Because the Dodo wins every year | Because the animals all fall asleep
